# Testing

## Overview

Most Jido tests need no process. `cmd/3` runs a route and returns the
candidate state and Directives as data, so you assert on values. Tests that
need a live Server start their own Jido instance under the test supervisor,
which keeps them independent and safe to run with `async: true`. This
Livebook runs each kind of test with ExUnit.

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido, "~> 3.0.0-beta.2"}])
```

```elixir
ExUnit.start(autorun: false)
```

In a project, `test/test_helper.exs` calls `ExUnit.start()` and `mix test`
runs the files. Here, `ExUnit.run/0` runs the modules defined above it.

## The Code Under Test

A Plugin that counts Turns, and a counter Agent that uses it:

```elixir
defmodule Shop.TurnCount do
  use Jido.Plugin

  alias Jido.Agent.Plugin.Reduction

  @impl true
  def state_spec(_opts) do
    {:turns, Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.min(0) |> Zoi.default(0)}
  end

  @impl true
  def reduce(%Reduction{plugin_state: turns}, _opts), do: {:ok, turns + 1}
end

defmodule Shop.Counter do
  use Jido.Agent,
    name: "shop_counter",
    extensions: [Shop.TurnCount]

  agent do
    schema(Zoi.object(%{count: Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.default(0)}))
  end

  routes do
    signal_source("/shop")

    route "counter.increment", as: :increment, defaults: %{amount: 1} do
      action %{amount: amount},
        schema: Zoi.object(%{amount: Zoi.integer() |> Zoi.positive()}),
        context: context do
        count = context.agent_state.count + amount

        {:ok, %{context.agent_state | count: count},
         [Jido.Agent.Directive.emit("counter.changed", %{count: count})]}
      end
    end
  end
end
```

## Test Routes With cmd/3

`cmd/3` runs in the test process. Build an instance, send a Signal, and match
on the result:

```elixir
defmodule Shop.CounterTest do
  use ExUnit.Case, async: true

  alias Jido.Agent.Directive.Emit

  @moduletag :capture_log

  setup do
    %{agent: Shop.Counter.instantiate!(id: "counter-1")}
  end

  test "increment adds the amount", %{agent: agent} do
    signal = Shop.Counter.increment_signal!(%{amount: 3})

    assert {:ok, candidate, [%Emit{signal: emitted}]} = Shop.Counter.cmd(agent, signal)
    assert candidate.state.count == 3
    assert emitted.type == "counter.changed"
    assert emitted.data == %{count: 3}
  end

  test "the input agent is unchanged", %{agent: agent} do
    signal = Shop.Counter.increment_signal!(%{amount: 3})
    {:ok, _candidate, _} = Shop.Counter.cmd(agent, signal)

    assert agent.state.count == 0
  end

  test "invalid input fails before any change", %{agent: agent} do
    signal = Shop.Counter.increment_signal!(%{amount: -1})

    assert {:error, _reason} = Shop.Counter.cmd(agent, signal)
  end

  test "an unknown type has no route", %{agent: agent} do
    signal = Jido.Signal.new!("counter.unknown", %{}, source: "/test")

    assert {:error, error} = Shop.Counter.cmd(agent, signal)
    assert error.message == "No route accepts this Signal type"
  end
end
```

Directives come back as structs. Match their fields. `cmd/3` doesn't run
them, so an `Emit` sends nothing during the test.

A failed Action logs a warning. `@moduletag :capture_log` keeps that output
out of the test report and prints it only when a test fails.

## Test a Plugin

A Plugin runs inside `cmd/3` too: `prepare/2` before the route and
`reduce/2` after it. Test it through an Agent that uses it, and assert on
the field it owns:

```elixir
defmodule Shop.TurnCountTest do
  use ExUnit.Case, async: true

  @moduletag :capture_log

  test "each successful Turn adds one" do
    agent = Shop.Counter.instantiate!(id: "counter-1")
    signal = Shop.Counter.increment_signal!(%{amount: 1})

    {:ok, agent, _} = Shop.Counter.cmd(agent, signal)
    {:ok, agent, _} = Shop.Counter.cmd(agent, signal)

    assert agent.state.turns == 2
  end

  test "a failed Turn doesn't count" do
    agent = Shop.Counter.instantiate!(id: "counter-1")
    bad = Shop.Counter.increment_signal!(%{amount: 0})

    {:error, _} = Shop.Counter.cmd(agent, bad)
    assert agent.state.turns == 0
  end
end
```

`admit/3`, `after_commit/3`, and Plugin runtimes run only on a live Server.
Test them with the live setup below.

## One Instance Per Test

A live test starts a Jido instance with `start_supervised/1`. ExUnit stops it
when the test ends. Give each instance a unique name, so async tests never
share a registry:

```elixir
defmodule Shop.LiveCase do
  def start_jido(opts \\ []) do
    name = :"jido_test_#{System.unique_integer([:positive])}"
    {:ok, _pid} = ExUnit.Callbacks.start_supervised({Jido, Keyword.put(opts, :name, name)})
    name
  end
end
```

Pass the instance name as the first argument to `Jido.start_agent/3` and the
other instance functions.

## Wait Without Sleeping

Every check below waits on a message or a reply. None of them use
`Process.sleep/1`:

- `call/3` returns after commit. Assert on the committed Agent it returns.
- `cast/2` returns at once. A later `call/3` or `status/1` runs after the
  cast's Turn, because Turns are serial.
- Emitted Signals run after the reply. Send them to the test process with
  `default_dispatch: {:pid, target: self()}` and use `assert_receive`.

```elixir
defmodule Shop.LiveCounterTest do
  use ExUnit.Case, async: true

  setup do
    %{jido: Shop.LiveCase.start_jido()}
  end

  test "a call commits and the Emit arrives", %{jido: jido} do
    {:ok, pid} =
      Jido.start_agent(jido, Shop.Counter,
        id: "counter-1",
        default_dispatch: {:pid, target: self()}
      )

    signal = Shop.Counter.increment_signal!(%{amount: 2})

    assert {:ok, agent, [_emit]} = Jido.AgentServer.call(pid, signal)
    assert agent.state.count == 2
    assert_receive {:signal, %Jido.Signal{type: "counter.changed", data: %{count: 2}}}
  end

  test "a cast is visible to the next call", %{jido: jido} do
    {:ok, pid} =
      Jido.start_agent(jido, Shop.Counter,
        id: "counter-1",
        default_dispatch: {:pid, target: self()}
      )

    signal = Shop.Counter.increment_signal!(%{amount: 1})

    :ok = Jido.AgentServer.cast(pid, signal)
    {:ok, agent, _} = Jido.AgentServer.call(pid, signal)

    assert agent.state.count == 2
  end
end
```

Both tests use the ID `"counter-1"`. They run at the same time without a
conflict because each has its own instance.

## Persistent Agents

Give the test instance a namespace and an ETS table of its own. ETS records
live in memory and end with the BEAM, so nothing persists between test runs:

```elixir
defmodule Shop.DurableCounterTest do
  use ExUnit.Case, async: true

  setup do
    n = System.unique_integer([:positive])

    jido =
      Shop.LiveCase.start_jido(
        namespace: "test/#{n}",
        persistence: {Jido.Persistence.ETS, table: :"shop_test_#{n}"}
      )

    %{jido: jido}
  end

  test "state survives hibernate and thaw", %{jido: jido} do
    {:ok, pid} =
      Jido.start_agent(jido, Shop.Counter,
        id: "counter-1",
        persistence: :inherit,
        default_dispatch: {:pid, target: self()}
      )

    :ok = Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(pid)
    signal = Shop.Counter.increment_signal!(%{amount: 5})
    {:ok, _agent, _} = Jido.AgentServer.call(pid, signal)

    :ok = Jido.hibernate(jido, pid)
    refute Process.alive?(pid)

    {:ok, thawed} = Jido.thaw(jido, Shop.Counter, "counter-1")
    :ok = Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(thawed)
    agent = Jido.AgentServer.agent(thawed)

    assert agent.state.count == 5
    assert agent.state.turns == 1
  end
end
```

A persistent Agent writes its first record before it reports ready.
`start_agent/3` and `thaw/3` return before that, so call
`Jido.AgentServer.await_ready/2` before the first Signal. A call sent too
early returns `{:error, :not_ready}`. `hibernate/2` returns after the record
is saved and the Server has stopped.

## Run the Tests

```elixir
ExUnit.run()
```

The result is `%{total: 9, failures: 0, excluded: 0, skipped: 0}`. ExUnit
stopped each test instance, so no Agent or Jido instance from these tests is
still running:

```elixir
Enum.filter(Process.registered(), &String.starts_with?(Atom.to_string(&1), "jido_test_"))
```

The result is `[]`.

